diagnosis_text:
Affected component: pc0’s host routing configuration (default gateway/neighbor resolution). Mechanism: Any destination outside 20.0.0.0/8 should be sent to the default next hop. pc0 is configured with “default via 20.1.1.99 dev eth0,” but that next hop does not exist on the LAN. As a result, when pc0 tries to reach off-subnet destinations (e.g., 10.1.1.1 or 120.1.1.10), it ARPs for 20.1.1.99, fails, and the kernel immediately returns “Destination Host Unreachable” sourced from 20.1.1.10. Localization: On pc0, “ip route” shows the default via 20.1.1.99; “ip neigh” shows “20.1.1.99 dev eth0 FAILED,” while the actual local router r1 at 20.1.1.1 is reachable and present in the ARP cache (“20.1.1.1 … REACHABLE”). On r1, the LAN interface is 20.1.1.1/8 and participates in OSPF, so upstream reachability is healthy. Root cause: pc0’s default gateway is misconfigured to a nonexistent IP (20.1.1.99) instead of the connected router (20.1.1.1). Corrective fix: On pc0, remove the bad default route and add the correct one via 20.1.1.1 (e.g., ip route del default; ip route add default via 20.1.1.1 dev eth0), flush the failed neighbor entry (ip neigh flush dev eth0), and persist the change in the host’s network config. After correction, pings to 10.1.1.1 (OSPF backbone) and 120.1.1.10 (pc12) should succeed via r1. === END ===